# Target C — Section-162 endpoint gap extension to `eta=9/10000`

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** proof-only arithmetic extension of audited-working Section 162.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The audited-working Section-162 endpoint argument proves a Type-II density gap above

\[
C_{146}:=\frac49\left(\pi+\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}\right)
=1.8195135379794445\ldots .
\]

Its published choice `eta=1/1250` is conservative.  Repeating exactly the same
near-Mantel and atom-versus-detour argument with

\[
\boxed{\eta_0=\frac9{10000}}
\]

still leaves a strict rational margin.  Therefore, under the same audited-working
dependencies and scope as Section 162,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}\frac{q-k}{D}
\ge C_{146}+\frac9{10000}
=1.8204135379794445\ldots .
}
\tag{E.1}
\]

This is a proved candidate awaiting promotion only as an arithmetic strengthening;
it does not close the full-core positive-slack regime or the Type-I inactive-container
branch.

## 1. The inherited implication chain

Assume, along a Type-II subsequence, that

\[
t_D:=\frac{q-k}{D}\le C_{146}+\eta_0+o(1).
\tag{E.2}
\]

Use the audited-working Section-146 endpoint stability inequalities exactly as in
Section 162.  With `s=sqrt(pi/(2 sqrt(3)))`, they give

\[
0\le\frac{k}{D}\le9\eta_0+o(1),
\qquad
\frac{b-\operatorname{per}(F)}D\le\frac94\eta_0+o(1).
\tag{E.3}
\]

Let `A` be the absolute `D`-density of atomic stopped-train phase mass, and let `N`
be its nonatomic mass.  The same train ledger and weighted Mantel estimates give

\[
2s\le A\le A_0+o(1),
\qquad
A_0:=2s+\frac92\eta_0,
\tag{E.4}
\]

and the mass outside one dominant three-axis phase orbit is at most

\[
R_0:=A_0-\frac{4s^2}{A_0}.
\tag{E.5}
\]

The outer-terminal occurrence ledger then gives one outer direction atom of mass at
least

\[
\ell_0:=
\frac{\pi-C_{146}-10\eta_0-2R_0}{3}.
\tag{E.6}
\]

The Section-162 atom-versus-detour lemma says that an outer direction atom of mass
`ell` forces normalized projection excess at least

\[
\mathcal E(\ell):=\frac{\ell^3}{2\pi^2+\ell^2}.
\tag{E.7}
\]

On the other hand (E.3) bounds that same excess by `(9/2)eta_0`.  Thus (E.2) is
impossible whenever

\[
\mathcal E(\ell_0)>\frac92\eta_0.
\tag{E.8}
\]

This is the complete candidate implication chain:

```text
E.2 -> E.3--E.6 -> E.7--E.8 -> contradiction -> E.1.
```

No new geometric premise is introduced.

## 2. Exact rational certificate

For the certificate, use the rational enclosures

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{3141592653589793}{10^{15}}&<\pi<
\frac{3141592653589794}{10^{15}},\\
\frac{1732050807568877}{10^{15}}&<\sqrt3<
\frac{1732050807568878}{10^{15}},\\
\frac{9523128}{10^7}&<s<\frac{9523129}{10^7}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{E.9}
\]

The first two are standard Machin-series/rational-square enclosures.  The last
follows from the first two because

\[
\left(\frac{9523128}{10^7}\right)^2
<\frac{\pi_{lo}}{2\sqrt3_{hi}},
\qquad
\left(\frac{9523129}{10^7}\right)^2
>\frac{\pi_{hi}}{2\sqrt3_{lo}}.
\]

Write `c=(9/2)eta_0` and

\[
R(s)=A_0-\frac{4s^2}{A_0}
     =c\frac{4s+c}{2s+c}.
\tag{E.10}
\]

The last expression is increasing in `s`.  Consequently the lower and upper
rational evaluations

\[
\ell_{lo}=\frac{5\pi_{lo}/9-4s_{hi}/9-10\eta_0-2R(s_{hi})}{3},
\]

\[
\ell_{hi}=\frac{5\pi_{hi}/9-4s_{lo}/9-10\eta_0-2R(s_{lo})}{3}
\]

enclose `ell_0`.  The certificate obtains

\[
\ell_{lo}>0.4322987538,
\qquad
\ell_{hi}<0.4322987687,
\]

and hence

\[
\frac{\ell_0^3}{2\pi^2+\ell_0^2}
\ge
\frac{\ell_{lo}^3}{2\pi_{hi}^2+\ell_{hi}^2}
>0.0040544303
>0.00405=\frac92\eta_0.
\tag{E.11}
\]

The strict margin is greater than `4.4e-6` under these deliberately short rational
enclosures.  Run

```sh
python3 research/verify_target_c_section162_gap_extension.py
```

to reproduce the exact Fraction comparison.

## 3. Scope and classification

- The argument imports the audited-working Section-146 stability inequalities and the
  audited-working Section-162 atom-versus-detour lemma without changing either scope.
- It uses occurrence-counted outer terminals and retains contact-isolated source
  points; no full-source affine-kernel statement is used.
- The result is a scalar endpoint separation.  It supplies no upper bound for the
  surviving positive-slack interval and no source-preserving deformation.
- The Type-I inactive-container branch is untouched.

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion.  The theorem-state lock,
canonical route map, and generated state views remain unchanged.
